<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian National Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Congress movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian National Congress is a political party founded in 1885 that became the leading organisation of the Indian independence movement. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> it organised mass campaigns against British rule. Its struggle contributed to the end of British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress.jpg" alt="Indian National Congress" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original design of flag &amp; emblem by Indian National Congress.Vectorized version by Swapnil1101. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_National_Congress_hand_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>During the later 1870s Indians made concerted efforts to found a pan-Indian organisation with nationalist political influence. In 1883 Allan Octavian Hume, a retired British civil servant known for his pro-Indian activities, set out the idea of a body representing Indian interests in an open letter to graduates of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. Its aim was to win a greater share in government for educated Indians. A meeting planned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> because of a cholera outbreak, and the first session of the Congress was held there from 28 to 31 December 1885. Hume organised it with the approval of the Viceroy, Lord Dufferin, and served as general secretary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From its start the Congress was led by Moderates influenced by Western liberalism, who demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> and equality regardless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, creed or sex. By 1905 it had divided in two. The Moderates, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gopal-krishna-gokhale">Gopal Krishna Gokhale</a>, sought self-government by peaceful, constitutional means within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. The Extremists, led by Lala Lajpat Rai, Bipin Chandra Pal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a>, favoured a more confrontational course.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1915 and joined the Congress. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the party came to be associated with him as its unofficial spiritual leader. In 1920 he allied with the Khilafat Movement against British rule, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>, or satyagraha, as his tool, and in 1922 he suspended the agitation after policemen were killed at Chauri Chaura.</p><p>After independence in 1947 the Congress dominated Indian politics. In the first general election of 1952 it won 364 of 479 contested seats, and it held power nationally until 1977. Its first prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, led it to support a mixed economy, the Planning Commission and Five-Year Plans. In 1969 the party split, and from 1975 to 1977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> declared a state of emergency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party has governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for more than 54 years, with six prime ministers from its ranks, the first being Nehru. Under Nehru it oversaw the founding of institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-institutes-of-technology">Indian Institutes of Technology</a> and the All India Institute of Medical Sciences, and it helped shape India's non-aligned foreign policy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian National Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was achieved through the mass civil disobedience campaigns led by Gandhi and the <b>Indian National Congress</b> and the growing impossibility of governing some 400 million people who no longer consented to be governed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Raj’s classification of Indians by caste and religion in the census and its separate electorates hardened communal identities, while the <b>Indian National Congress</b>, founded in 1885, and the All-India Muslim League, founded in 1906, developed into the parties that led India and Pakistan to independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of India was the outcome of a national movement that had challenged British rule since the founding of the <b>Indian National Congress</b> in 1885 and, under Mahatma Gandhi from 1920, mobilised millions through campaigns of non-cooperation and civil disobedience.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1885 seventy-two delegates met in Bombay for the first session of the <b>Indian National Congress</b>, encouraged by a retired British official, Allan Octavian Hume, and presided over by the Calcutta barrister Womesh Chunder Bonnerjee.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…as Giovanni Sartori distinguish the one-party system, where no other party may compete, from the hegemonic party system, where minor parties exist but can never win, and from the predominant-party system, where one party keeps winning free and competitive elections, as the <b>Indian National Congress</b> did after independence.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>He urged members of the <b>Indian National Congress</b> to spend time each day at the charkha, the spinning wheel, partly to link privileged Brahmins and lawyers in Congress with the peasantry, and Motilal Nehru and others gave up Western dress for it.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/gandhism">Gandhism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
